From c0d92202bbccbf834fd55aa419b23460571a1263 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jmgasper <jmgasper@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 20:50:25 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 24/32] usb_raw: a device unplugged mid-transfer no longer
 hangs every /dev open

When a device went away with a control request in flight, device_removed
only cancelled the endpoints of the first interface. The request stayed
queued on the default pipe until Device::~Device() cancelled it by force,
which deletes transfers without calling their callbacks. The ioctl then
waited on the notify semaphore for good, holding the device lock, and
could not be killed out of it either.

usb_raw_free() of another descriptor for the same device then blocked on
that lock while holding gDeviceListLock, and the rescan after the removal
blocked on that inside legacy_driver_rescan(), holding the legacy driver
lock. Every open and close of a legacy device (/dev/null, /dev/tty, ptys,
/dev/urandom) takes that lock, so new ssh logins, Terminals, shells and
web processes all stopped. Seen on the X399 when a NetMD MiniDisc dropped
off USB while Amp was writing to it.

- device_removed cancels the default pipe's requests as well as the
  endpoints of every interface, so waiting ioctls get B_CANCELED.
- usb_raw_free() frees a removed device only when its last reference goes;
  it used to free it while other descriptors were still open.

Installed on the X399 in a repacked haiku package; USB, Terminal and
Summit work after it. Not yet tried against an unplug during a transfer.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../kernel/drivers/bus/usb/usb_raw.cpp        | 32 +++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/add-ons/kernel/drivers/bus/usb/usb_raw.cpp b/src/add-ons/kernel/drivers/bus/usb/usb_raw.cpp
index 517985ef2d..2f76b16705 100644
--- a/src/add-ons/kernel/drivers/bus/usb/usb_raw.cpp
+++ b/src/add-ons/kernel/drivers/bus/usb/usb_raw.cpp
@@ -98,17 +98,28 @@ usb_raw_device_removed(void *cookie)
 	raw_device *device = (raw_device *)cookie;
 
 	// cancel all pending transfers to make sure no one keeps waiting forever
-	// in syscalls.
+	// in syscalls. This has to happen here: once the device is freed, the
+	// stack cancels what is left by force, without calling the callbacks, and
+	// an ioctl waiting for one would never return while holding the device
+	// lock. That includes control requests on the default pipe.
+	gUSBModule->cancel_queued_requests(device->device);
+
 	const usb_configuration_info *configurationInfo =
 		gUSBModule->get_configuration(device->device);
-	if (configurationInfo != NULL && configurationInfo->interface_count > 0) {
-		// An interface the device described no alternate for has no active
-		// one.
-		struct usb_interface_info* interface
-			= configurationInfo->interface->active;
-		for (unsigned int i = 0;
-				interface != NULL && i < interface->endpoint_count; i++)
-			gUSBModule->cancel_queued_transfers(interface->endpoint[i].handle);
+	if (configurationInfo != NULL) {
+		for (size_t i = 0; i < configurationInfo->interface_count; i++) {
+			// An interface the device described no alternate for has no
+			// active one.
+			struct usb_interface_info* interface
+				= configurationInfo->interface[i].active;
+			if (interface == NULL)
+				continue;
+
+			for (size_t j = 0; j < interface->endpoint_count; j++) {
+				gUSBModule->cancel_queued_transfers(
+					interface->endpoint[j].handle);
+			}
+		}
 	}
 
 	mutex_lock(&gDeviceListLock);
@@ -235,7 +246,8 @@ usb_raw_free(void *cookie)
 
 	raw_device *device = (raw_device *)cookie;
 	device->reference_count--;
-	if (device->device == 0) {
+	if (device->device == 0 && device->reference_count == 0) {
+		// the device is gone and this was its last user
 		mutex_lock(&device->lock);
 		mutex_destroy(&device->lock);
 		delete_sem(device->notify);
-- 
